      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence is creating a major impact on higher education, with tools like ChatGPT (Chat Generative Pre-trained Transformer) changing how students learn, teachers teach, and even how students are assessed (Dwivedi et al., 2023;Rudolph et al., 2023). This phenomenon has drawn attention of researchers on the potential applications of chatbots in education (Haenlein & Kaplan, 2020). ChatGPT is a form of generative artificial intelligence (AI) that can have conversations like a real person, can answer student questions right away, making it a valuable asset in education (Foroughi et al., 2023;Strzelecki, 2023). Many studies (Abbas et al., 2022;Pillai et al., 2023) have identified benefits of ChatGPT for teaching and learning in higher education. For example, it can tailor learning to individual students, keep them engaged, and give them quick access to information and feedback (Abbas et al., 2022;Pillai et al., 2023). It can even help with personalized support (Mijwil & Aljanabi, 2023), accomplish homework assignments (Foroughi et al., 2023), answering tough questions, grading work, and sparking discussions (Cotton et al., 2023). This suggest that ChatGPT can provide innovative approaches in teaching and learning by solving complex educational problems for teachers and students in higher education. Recently, the adoption and usage of ChatGPT in higher education has seen unprecedented growth (Pillai et al., 2023). Hence, investigating students' intention to use this new AI technology is therefore vital (Bonsu & Baffour-Koduah, 2023;Menon & Shilpa, 2023).",
