      "snippet": {
        "text": "Wardat et al. postulated that these issues might stem from the complexity of the input, the instructions given to ChatGPT, and the inherent limitations of large language models in understanding abstract mathematical reasoning. \n\nRegarding problem-solving (see also Jia et al., 2024;Rane, 2023), Dao and Le (2023) provided a complete analysis of ChatGPT's mathematics abilities in responding to multiple-choice questions for the Vietnamese National High School Graduation Examination (VNHSGE) on a range of subjects and levels of difficulty. Results showed that ChatGPT's performance varied depending on the difficulty level and subject: it performed best on questions at the knowledge level, but as the level of difficulty rose, it scored poorly, going from 82% down to 10%. In their study, ChatGPT performed particularly well on questions on subjects such as exponential and logarithmic functions, geometric progression, and arithmetic progression. The study found that ChatGPT had difficulty correctly answering questions on topics including derivatives and applications, spatial geometry, and areas of spatial calculus. These results suggest that \"ChatGPT has the potential to be an effective teaching tool for mathematics, but more work is needed to enhance its handling of graphical data and address the challenges presented by questions that are getting more challenging\" (p. 1). Similarly, Hendrycks et al. (2021) tested GPT-3 on various mathematical problems, including arithmetic, algebra, calculus, and word problems, to assess its accuracy and reasoning skills. The results showed that while GPT-3 performed well on simple arithmetic and pattern recognition tasks, it struggles with more complex problems requiring deeper mathematical reasoning. The model often produced incorrect or inconsistent answers, highlighting limitations in its ability to handle intricate mathematical concepts. The study suggests that. while GPT-3 demonstrated some mathematical understanding, significant improvements are needed for it to be reliably used in educational or professional mathematical contexts. \n\nFor utilizing ChatGPT for mathematical reasoning and proof, the study by Collins et al. ( 2023) examines the capabilities of large language models (LLMs) in performing mathematical reasoning and generating formal proofs. The study found that while LLMs can successfully mimic some aspects of logical reasoning and produce simple proofs, they often fail in more complex or abstract scenarios.",
