      "snippet": {
        "text": "This ensured that all students were engaged and challenged at their own levels (Gurl et al. this issue). The Australian study by Cameron and Mesiti (2024) explored the pedagogical practices embedded in lesson plans generated by ChatGPT, focusing on the topic of dividing fractions. The study involved analyzing four AI-generated lesson plans to identify the instructional strategies and educational approaches they promoted. The findings revealed that while ChatGPT-produced lesson plans were structurally consistent and included common instructional techniques, like guided practice and worked examples, they often lacked the depth and contextual understanding that experienced teachers would bring to lesson planning. The study suggested that while ChatGPT could assist in generating lesson plans, it did not replace the nuanced pedagogical expertise required for effective teaching. \n\nChatGPT has also been shown to be able to support pre-service teachers in acquiring and practicing planning, reflection, and analysis skills. Wardat et al. (2023) reported how pre-service teachers could utilize ChatGPT to design and implement innovative instructional strategies and to receive feedback on their teaching practices. In a university course on mathematics education, Buchholtz and Huget (2024) made ChatGPT available to pre-service teachers as a dialogic AI assistant for lesson planning and adapting mathematics tasks. ChatGPT could pose reflective questions during the planning process, promoting critical thinking and optimization of lesson plans. During lesson planning in narrowly defined prompt settings, questions that the generative AI asked the pre-service teachers within the planning dialog (e.g., about material learning objectives, methodological design, or didactic principles that should be considered) proved to be particularly valuable for developing lesson planning-related skills. The questions asked supported the pre-service teachers in critically reflecting on and optimizing their own planning, which proved to be essential for the development of planning-related skills and the promotion of reflection processes. \n\nMoreover, it has been shown that ChatGPT could provide explanations and clarifications for complex mathematical concepts, aiding in the teacher's understanding and ability to convey the material effectively. In a study by Israeli researchers, Bagno et al. (2023) explored how pre-service teachers benefit from using ChatGPT to expand their mathematical knowledge and engage in interactive learning experiences.",
