        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study explored students' perceptions of ChatGPT and examined how contrasting perceptions influence their utilization of ChatGPT in a college-level calculus class. The results align with the technology models in the literature, which suggests that student perceptions are a valid predictor of actual usage (Poellhuber et al., 2018), learner satisfaction (Nagy, 2018), and learning motivation (Hsu, 2012). This study extends prior research by highlighting that student perceptions play a key role in their acceptance or rejection of learning with AI tools like ChatGPT, particularly within the context of mathematical learning. However, this study did not explore factors such as the educational context, the accuracy of ChatGPT's responses, and students' proficiency in crafting prompts. Future research should investigate external and internal factors (e.g., psychological factors) to better understand students' adoption of AI tools in mathematical learning. \n\nDue to the limited scope of focusing solely on students with either positive or negative perceptions of ChatGPT, future studies could investigate a broader range of students-including those with neutral or evolving views-to gain deeper insights into how they engage with ChatGPT in mathematical learning. In addition, this paper introduced the RQ approach to explicitly teach students how to evaluate ChatGPT's mathematical solutions critically. By guiding students to ask rational questions that address the epistemic, teleological, and communicative dimensions of ChatGPT's outputs, the RQ apporach facilitates discussions and reflections on various aspects of rational behavior in mathematical activities. Future research can explore how this framework may support students' awareness of the rationality components in validating ChatGPT-generated mathematical content during a long-term intervention. Moreover, this study only followed limited students for one semester with six lab assignments using ChatGPT. A future study could implement a longitudinal design to track changes in students' attitudes and engagement with ChatGPT over an extended learning period. This approach would enable a deeper analysis of how sustained exposure to AI tools, combined with tailored interventions such as instruction on using ChatGPT and personalized timely feedback on students' interactions with ChatGPT might shift students' negative perceptions. Furthermore, incorporating a larger and more diverse sample could offer valuable insights into how other factors such as prior knowledge may influence student engagement with AI in mathematics education.",
