      "snippet": {
        "text": "In schools and universities, ChatGPT has the potential to offer personalized and effective learning experiences by providing students with tailored feedback and explanations, as well as creating realistic virtual simulations for hands-on learning (Zhang & Tur, 2024;Kasneci et al., 2023;Miao & Holmes, 2023). In the education community, some see it as a tool to enhance learning and reduce teacher workload (Celik et al., 2022;Hashem et al., 2024), while others see it as a threat to integrity which opens the door to cheating and plagiarism (Dunnigan et al., 2023;Lee et al., 2024;Susnjak & McIntosh, 2024). Also significant for the subject of mathematics, in particular, is the challenge of dealing with the misinformation that ChatGPT can provide, including withholding essential information, rambling, or hallucinating (Frieder et al., 2023;Rawte et al., 2023). For example, we refer to the study by Frieder et al., (2023, p. 29). As there appear to be both benefits and challenges of using ChatGPT (or AI in general), it is important to investigate the limitations of this technology. \n\nIn this article, we present a scoping review of the research literature that reports on studies involving ChatGPT in mathematics (and, to some extent, general) education. This scoping review includes the research literature that provides the basis of the articles in this special issue. The special issue itself is designed to feature current research studies that take a closer look at ChatGPT to develop insights into its importance and use in school and university mathematics education. At the same time, ChatGPT is (technologically) developing faster than research on ChatGPT can be conducted and published, and hence, this special issue can only be seen as one of the early attempts to address ChatGPT's influence on mathematics education.",
