          "status": "HYBRID",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1007/s10639-025-13369-z?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-025-13369-z, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Recent research focusing on chatbot technology shows that it has been successfully applied in various fields (Okonkwo & Ade-Ibijola, 2021;Wollny et al., 2021). Chatbots utilize AI or deep learning-based models and implement feedback through text or voice interfaces (Winkler & Söllner, 2018). \n\nChatbots used as tutors epitomize open-ended tutoring, a departure from the closed-end tutoring model, which predominantly focuses on arriving at correct solutions to specific problems (Alam, 2022(Alam, , 2023)). Through sophisticated natural language processing and machine learning algorithms, these bots facilitate interactive dialogues with students, seeking to furnish personalized aid and resources aligned with specific learning objectives (Fauzi et al., 2023;Rasul et al., 2023). \n\nBy seamlessly integrating adaptive learning elements (Alam, 2022;Rane et al., 2023;Shih et al., 2021), AI systems are designed to further enhance the efficacy of personalized instruction. Adaptive learning algorithms dynamically adjust instructional content and strategies based on individual learner progress and performance (Kabudi et al., 2021;Lee et al., 2022;Lo, 2023). To educators, instructional designers, AI researchers, and developers, this fusion of AI-driven personalization and adaptive learning holds the promise of augmenting the transformative potential of educational technologies in enhancing the learning experiences (Chang et al., 2023). \n\nAccording to Pardos and Bhandari (2023) and Saetra (2022), recent bots powered by generative AI technologies can leverage their knowledge from Language Model (LLM) training to enhance their effectiveness compared to human tutors. However, there are still challenges and implications of LLM that herald to understanding how and to what extent these technologies can reshape space of possibilities for PL (Grassini, 2023;Whalen & Mouza, 2023).",
