      "snippet": {
        "text": "In contextualsettings, Pahm et al. stated that although the questions produced by ChatGPT were grammatically correct and contextually appropriate, they were generally lower in difficulty than expected and that ChatGPT could produce more effective questions when guided by example demonstrations. Rycroft-Smith and Macey (2024) compared the ability of three different large language models, Copilot, Claude, and ChatGPT3.5, to write questions about the area of a rectangle. ChatGPT provided more multi-part questions and scoring opportunities, which increased the probability of the language model making errors. The study emphasizes that language models should be carefully evaluated in educational material design (Rycroft-Smith & Macey, 2024). In the studies, ChatGPT had a more limited performance in mathematics education. However, updating artificial intelligence to increase its potential daily was an important performance issue. Thus, this study aims to evaluate its measurement and evaluation potential in mathematics education within the scope of a test controlled by item analysis. In this context, the study was carried out using the number sense achievement test created by Chat-GPT. One of the main reasons for conducting the research within the framework of this subject is that number sense tests have more thought-provoking and complex question structures and aim to measure students' understanding of basic mathematical knowledge and high-level thinking skills (Greeno, 1989). Therefore, a number sense test was developed to understand ChatGPT's performance in creating complex questions. At the same time, the contributions that ChatGPT can make for researchers in the development process of achievement test, its limitations, and the quality of the test created were evaluated. The research aims to provide a new perspective to researchers by evaluating the potential of ChatGPT in achievement test development. As a result of the literature review, apart from a few studies evaluating the question writing performance of ChatGPT (Barros, 2024;Guler et al., 2024;Jia et al., 2024;Ngo et al., 2024), no study on achievement test development for this purpose was found. The study's findings would provide a new perspective on the literature regarding the limits and capabilities of ChatGPT for achievement test development and filling the gap in the field.",
