          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1007/s40751-025-00172-1?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1007/s40751-025-00172-1, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "In this section, we draw our conclusions with respect to the research question: \n\nAccording to the literature, what are the implications (benefits and challenges) of teachers' and students' interaction with ChatGPT for their teaching and learning of mathematics? \n\nWhile some opponents argue that AI has become just the latest technology embraced by those advocating a neo-progressive vision of education (e.g., Cuban, 1989), we contend that the incorporation of AI technologies, with a particular focus on generative AI models such as ChatGPT, has been accompanied by a revolutionary atmosphere in the field of education, also in mathematics education, fundamentally altering the ways students engage with mathematical problem-solving. By implementing these cutting-edge technologies, it has been argued, educators can potentially provide tailored learning experiences that cater to a wide array of learning experiences and paces. \n\nThis was said potentially to enable students to receive immediate feedback, participate in interactive problem-solving dialogues, and access step-by-step solutions that align with their individual requirements. At the same time, it has become evident that students themselves can access and increasingly use technologies, such as ChatGPT, to support their personalized learning, as ChatGPT can be used to create interactive learning experiences that are tailored to individual students' preferences and needs. Moreover, ChatGPT may help students improve their language skills (e.g., writing) by providing instant feedback and suggestions or by adopting the role of a text conversation partner. Also, ChatGPT can be used to tutor students in particular content areas. \n\nNevertheless, the integration of ChatGPT into mathematics education is not without challenges. Besides ethical issues (e.g., privacy, bias, and the potential for overreliance on technology, which we have not dealt with in this survey), it has been shown that a central challenge revolves around ensuring the accuracy and dependability of AI-generated solutions, mitigating any potential biases, and aiming to maintain a harmonious balance between automation and human intervention in the learning process. Moreover, it can be argued that ChatGPT may have cognitive impacts on students, questioning whether and how their reliance on technology might affect critical thinking skills and their grasp of fundamental concepts.",
