        "text": "The mathematics proficiency rates of high school students in the United States indicate low rates of preparedness for higher education (ACT, 2009), lag significantly behind those of top-scoring countries in international comparisons (Baldi, Jin, Skemer, Green, & Herget, 2007;National Center for Education Statistics, 2008), and have failed to show significant improvement for almost a generation (National Center for Education Statistics, 2009). To improve mathematics learning in our nation's secondary schools, many educators have called for curriculum and teaching methods that emphasize active learning, build on prior knowledge, and apply mathematical concepts to real-world problems (National Council of Teachers of Mathematics, 2000). Moreover, there is empirical evidence suggesting that technology-based curricula can help personalize students' learning experiences and facilitate the development of mathematical skills (Koedinger, Corbett, Ritter, & Shapiro, 2000;Ritter, Kulikowich, Lei, McGuire, & Morgan, 2007;Schacter, 1999;Wenglinsky, 1998). However, much of the evidence about the benefits of technology for mathematics learning comes from quasi-experimental or small experimental studies, with limited data from large-scale, randomized trials to guide educational decision making. Rigorous evaluation of technology-based curricula is therefore needed if educators are to make informed curricular choices. This study addresses that need through a randomized, controlled trial designed to evaluate the efficacy of the Cognitive Tutor Geometry curriculum, published by Carnegie Learning, Inc.",
