      "snippet": {
        "text": "We developed a dataset comprising 55 different misconceptions and common errors (MaE), each accompanied by four diagnostic examples, resulting in a total of 220 examples. The dataset was created using a snowball sampling method [78], analyzing 145 peer-reviewed journal manuscripts, conference proceedings and articles, books, and dissertations. The misconceptions are characterized by dimensions such as algebra topic and the description of the misconception. The topics covered are based on the categorization [67,[79][80][81][82][83] and include algebra-related MaEs observed during fourth or fifth grade, as these can impact students' understanding of middle school algebra. \n\nOur dataset is specifically structured to be compatible with LLMs, enabling them to recognize patterns in student errors across various algebra topics and match student responses to known misconceptions more accurately. We focus on algebra because it is a critical prerequisite for success in high school, postsecondary education, and STEM careers [84,85]. \n\nTo evaluate this benchmark's characteristics and applications, we conducted a twopart study: \n\n1. LLM Performance Evaluation We measured GPT4-turbo1 ability to identify misconceptions using our benchmark with two standard metrics [86] precision (how many of the misconceptions identified by the LLM were correct) and recall (LLM's ability to find all relevant instances of the misconception). 2. Educator Feedback We invited feedback from algebra middle school educators on the LLM's errors to understand where and why the model might be struggling. We also surveyed these educators about the clarity of misconceptions in our benchmark and how often they encounter these misconceptions in their classrooms. Although the demographics of the research in the source papers were not always explicit, we validated the dataset by surveying five educators who serve low-income and minority students. These educators confirmed the relevance of misconceptions in their classrooms, ensuring the applicability of the benchmark to underserved populations.",
