        "text": "The larger effort surrounding Word Generation is an attempt to influence student learning, teacher learning, and school coherence simultaneously. Clearly, the effect of a program that lasts only 75 min a week cannot be expected to be enormous. Thus, our goal was that Word Generation would ultimately influence teachers by providing a scaffolded context for learning and practicing new pedagogical strategies useful in teaching vocabulary and fomenting productive classroom discussion. Nonetheless, in this article we report only immediate effects on students participating in the program, acknowledging that the basis for strong causal inference about program impact is limited in this quasiexperimental study. We posed three research questions: \n\n1. Did students in the schools implementing Word Generation learn the words taught? 2. Was there a differential effect of Word Generation on LM and EO students? 3. Did the learning promoted by Word Generation relate to performance on external achievement measures?",
