        "text": "Children should engage their EF skills during math learning and problem solving, and individual differences in EF are often correlated with math achievement (Bull & Lee, 2014;Clements et al., 2016). However, the current findings support more nuanced and contingent relations between EF and achievement. Our findings align with others that individual differences in EF skills were not predictive of math achievement in secondary school when prior math achievement was also included (Ahmed et al., 2019;Geary et al., 2017;Watts et al., 2015). Our findings are consistent with a \"fade-out\" model, where individual differences in EF skills become less relevant in upper elementary school and beyond, as differences in domain knowledge and skills play a much stronger role in supporting learning (Stipek & Valentino, 2015;Zhang et al., 2023). Our findings support the generalization of previous findings of fade-out from studies conducted with primarily White, middleclass students (Ahmed et al., 2019;Geary et al., 2017;Stipek & Valentino, 2015;Watts et al., 2015). Alternatively, rather than a fade-out model, individual differences in EF skills may not play a substantial and unique role even early in math development when appropriately accounting for other factors (Willoughby et al., 2019). This is an important area of future research.",
